body:
The transformations are derived by combining the \cref{Principle of relativity}{sr.principle_of_relativity} with the \cref{Constancy of the speed of light}{sr.constancy_of_speed_of_light}. Homogeneity of space and time implies that transformations between inertial coordinates are linear. The invariant light speed requires lightlike separations to remain lightlike in every inertial frame, and the stronger geometric statement is that the \cref{Spacetime interval}{sr.spacetime_interval} is preserved: \[ ds^2=c^2dt^2-dx^2-dy^2-dz^2=c^2dt'^2-dx'^2-dy'^2-dz'^2. \] In four-vector notation, with \(x^\mu=(ct,x,y,z)\), a Lorentz transformation is written \[ x'^\mu=\Lambda^\mu{}_{
u}\,x^
u, \] where \(\Lambda^\mu{}_{
u}\) are the components of the Lorentz transformation matrix. Preservation of the \cref{Metric tensor}{sr.metric_tensor} gives the defining condition \[ \eta_{\alpha eta}\Lambda^\alpha{}_{\mu}\,\Lambda^ eta{}_{
u}=\eta_{\mu
u}, \qquad \eta_{\mu
u}=\mathrm{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1). \] For a boost with relative speed \(v\) along the \(x\)-axis, this gives \[ ct'=\gamma(ct- eta x), \qquad x'=\gamma(x- eta ct), \qquad y'=y, \qquad z'=z, \] \optional_details{Check interval preservation for an x-boost}{Substitute the boost equations into \(c^2dt'^2-dx'^2\): \[ c^2dt'^2-dx'^2=\gamma^2[(cdt- eta dx)^2-(dx- eta cdt)^2]=\gamma^2(1- eta^2)(c^2dt^2-dx^2)=c^2dt^2-dx^2. \] The \(y\) and \(z\) coordinates are unchanged by this boost, so the full spacetime interval is preserved in the boosted frame.} where \( eta=v/c\) and \(\gamma=1/\sqrt{1- eta^2}\). \optional_details{Why homogeneity implies linear transformations}{Homogeneity means no special origin of space or time. If two displacements between events are the same in one inertial frame, they must transform in the same way wherever and whenever they occur. That rules out transformations whose coefficients depend on position or time, and it makes the transformation of a displacement depend linearly on the original displacement. Otherwise equal rods or equal clock intervals placed at different locations would transform differently.} \optional_details{From metric preservation to the x-boost equations}{For a boost along \(x\), symmetry says only \(ct\) and \(x\) mix, while \(y\) and \(z\) stay unchanged. Try \(ct'=a ct+b x\) and \(x'=d ct+e x\), then impose \(ct'^2-x'^2=ct^2-x^2\). The coefficients must satisfy \(a^2-d^2=1\), \(b^2-e^2=-1\), and \(ab-de=0\). Choosing the relative speed through the worldline \(x=vt\), which has \(x'=0\), gives the standard solution \(a=e=\gamma\) and \(b=d=-\gamma eta\).}

The concept is abstracted from the transformation law of the coordinate displacement \(\Delta x^\mu\). If \(\Delta x'^\mu=\Lambda^\mu{}_{
u}\,\Delta x^
u\), where \(\Lambda^\mu{}_{
u}\) are the components of the Lorentz transformation matrix, then any object \(A^\mu\) satisfying \[ A'^\mu=\Lambda^\mu{}_{
u}\,A^
u \] is called a four-vector. The \cref{Metric tensor}{sr.metric_tensor} then defines invariant scalar products, \[ A_\mu B^\mu=\eta_{\mu
u}A^\mu B^
u. \] For example, \(\Delta x_\mu\Delta x^\mu\) is the spacetime interval and \(p_\mu p^\mu=m^2c^2\) is the invariant square of four-momentum. \optional_details{Checking that \(A_\mu B^\mu\) is invariant}{Lower one index with the metric: \(A_\mu=\eta_{\mu
u}A^
u\). Then \(A_\mu B^\mu=A^T\eta B\) in matrix language. Under a Lorentz transformation this becomes \((\Lambda A)^T\eta(\Lambda B)=A^T\Lambda^T\eta\Lambda B\). Since \(\Lambda^T\eta\Lambda=\eta\), the scalar product is unchanged.} \optional_details{Components change, the four-vector does not}{A four-vector is a geometric object; its components are its description in a chosen inertial frame. When we change frames, the numbers \(A^0,A^1,A^2,A^3\) generally mix. The transformation law tells us how to update the components so that they still describe the same underlying spacetime object.}

A vector field attaches a vector-like object to every spacetime point. The electromagnetic vector potential is the main example here. The key is locality plus transformation: each event has its own components, and those components transform as a four-vector when observers change frames. It is different from drawing one big arrow in space. Better picture a spacetime grid with a small arrow anchored at each event, allowed to vary from event to event. This is the kind of object a local field equation can act on. Construction-wise, take the four-vector transformation law and apply it locally at every event. If \(A^\mu(x)\) is a vector field, then under a Lorentz transformation \[ A'^\mu(x')=\Lambda^\mu{}_{
u}\,A^
u(x). \] Thus a vector field is not four unrelated scalar fields; its components mix according to the same rule used for four-vectors. \optional_details{Why vector-field components mix at each event}{At each spacetime point, a vector field assigns a four-vector. When the observer changes inertial frame, that four-vector's components transform with \(\Lambda^\mu{}_{
u}\). The same mixing happens independently at each event, while the argument of the field also changes from \(x\) to \(x'\).} \optional_details{Why this is not four unrelated scalar fields}{Four unrelated scalar fields would transform as \(B'_0(x')=B_0(x)\), \(B'_1(x')=B_1(x)\), and so on, with no mixing. A vector field instead has components that combine under boosts and rotations. That mixing is what makes the four components one geometric object rather than four separate scalar quantities.}

Starting from the \cref{Vector potential}{sr.vector_potential}, take the antisymmetric spacetime derivative \[ F_{\mu
u}=\partial_\mu A_
u-\partial_
u A_\mu. \] The antisymmetry gives \(F_{\mu
u}=-F_{
u\mu}\), so in four dimensions there are six independent components. These components are identified, up to the conventional factors of \(c\) fixed by the choice of units, with the three components of the \cref{Electric field}{sr.electric_field} and the three components of the \cref{Magnetic field}{sr.magnetic_field}. Indices are raised and lowered with the \cref{Metric tensor}{sr.metric_tensor}. \optional_details{Counting the six independent components}{A four-by-four tensor has sixteen components. Antisymmetry says \(F_{\mu
u}=-F_{
u\mu}\), so the four diagonal components vanish because \(F_{\mu\mu}=-F_{\mu\mu}\). The off-diagonal components come in opposite pairs, leaving \(4\cdot3/2=6\) independent entries.} \optional_details{Mapping \(F_{\mu
u}\) to \(\mathbf E\) and \(\mathbf B\)}{After choosing an inertial frame, the components with one time index, such as \(F_{0i}\), are identified with electric-field components up to convention-dependent signs and factors of \(c\). The purely spatial components \(F_{ij}\) are antisymmetric in three dimensions, so they can be encoded by the three components of \(\mathbf B\). Different sign conventions move minus signs around, but the six physical components are the same.}

Starting from the \cref{Action principle}{sr.action_principle} with \cref{Minimal coupling}{sr.minimal_coupling}, varying the charged particle worldline gives the covariant force equation \[  rac{dp^\mu}{d	au}=eF^\mu{}_{
u}U^
u, \] up to conventional placement of indices and unit factors. Here \(p^\mu\) is the \cref{Momentum four-vector}{sr.momentum_four_vector}, \(U^
u\) is the \cref{Velocity four-vector}{sr.velocity_four_vector}, and \(F^\mu{}_{
u}\) is formed from the \cref{Field tensor}{sr.field_tensor}. In three-vector form this contains \[ \mathbf F=e(\mathbf E+\mathbf v	imes\mathbf B). \] \optional_details{Sketching the worldline variation}{The charged-particle action contains a free proper-time part and an interaction part proportional to \(eA_\mu dx^\mu\). Varying the path changes both the velocity and the value of \(A_\mu\) sampled along the path. After integration by parts, the derivative terms combine into \(\partial_\mu A_
u-\partial_
u A_\mu=F_{\mu
u}\), giving the covariant force law \(dp^\mu/d	au=eF^\mu{}_{
u}U^
u\).} \optional_details{Splitting the four-force into \(\mathbf E+\mathbf v	imes\mathbf B\)}{Choose an inertial frame and separate the time and spatial components of \(F^\mu{}_{
u}U^
u\). The time-space parts of \(F\) contribute the electric force, while the spatial antisymmetric parts contribute the cross product with \(\mathbf B\). The spatial part of the covariant equation then becomes the familiar three-vector expression \(d\mathbf p/dt=e(\mathbf E+\mathbf v	imes\mathbf B)\), up to unit conventions.}
